/* book Worked example: Q: The unaided eye can just separate two points about 0.1 mm (100 µm) apart at reading distance. (a) What is the smallest magnification at which a red blood cell 7.5 µm across can be seen as an object? (b) A bacterium 2 µm long? (c) If Leeuwenhoek's lens magnified 250 times, could he see bacteria? Could he see a ribosome about 25 nm across? (6 marks)
   Idea: An object becomes visible when its magnified image is at least as large as the smallest detail the eye can resolve: magnification × object size ≥ 100 µm. So the minimum magnification is M = 100 µm ÷ object size.
   1. Red blood cell: M = 100 µm ÷ 7.5 µm ≈ 13. About 13 times shows it as a dot; more is needed to see its shape.
   2. Bacterium: M = 100 µm ÷ 2 µm = 50 times.
   3. Leeuwenhoek and bacteria: image = 2 µm × 250 = 500 µm = 0.5 mm, five times the eye's limit. He could see bacteria as tiny moving specks, which is what he reported.
   4. Ribosome: 25 nm = 0.025 µm, so image = 0.025 µm × 250 = 6.25 µm, far below 100 µm. More magnification would not help: a light microscope cannot separate details closer than about 0.2 µm, because of the wavelength of light (Unit 6). Ribosomes had to wait for the electron microscope.
   Answer: (a) About 13 times. (b) 50 times. (c) Yes for bacteria (image 0.5 mm); no for ribosomes (image about 6 µm, and below the resolution limit of any light microscope). */
