/* book Worked example: Q: Treat a coccus (round bacterium) as a sphere 1 µm in diameter and an animal cell as a sphere 20 µm in diameter. (a) Find the surface area, volume and surface-area-to-volume ratio (SA/V) of each. (b) The time for a molecule to diffuse a distance x is roughly t ≈ x²/(2D), where D is its diffusion coefficient. How many times longer does a molecule take to diffuse from the membrane to the centre of the animal cell? (c) What does this mean for how the cells are organized? (8 marks)
   1. Bacterium, r = 0.5 µm: SA = 4π(0.5)² = 3.14 µm²; V = (4/3)π(0.5)³ = 0.524 µm³; SA/V = 3 ÷ 0.5 = 6.0 µm⁻¹.
   2. Animal cell, r = 10 µm: SA = 4π(10)² = 1257 µm²; V = (4/3)π(10)³ = 4189 µm³; SA/V = 3 ÷ 10 = 0.30 µm⁻¹.
   3. Compare: the animal cell has 8000 times the volume but only 400 times the surface, so each µm³ of it has 20 times less membrane serving it.
   4. Diffusion: D is the same for the same molecule, so t grows with x². Ratio = (10 µm ÷ 0.5 µm)² = 20² = 400.
   5. Meaning: a large cell cannot rely on its outer membrane and on diffusion alone. Eukaryotes add internal membranes (ER, mitochondrial cristae) to multiply membrane area, compartments that keep each job in one place, and motor proteins that carry cargo along the cytoskeleton.
   Answer: SA/V is 6.0 µm⁻¹ for the bacterium and 0.30 µm⁻¹ for the animal cell, 20 times less; diffusion to the centre takes about 400 times longer. Hence the compartments and active transport of eukaryotic cells. */
